---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE, IMAGES } from '../data/site.ts';

const title = 'Certifications & Compliance — ISO, Halal, HACCP, Pre-Shipment Inspection | Palude';
const description =
  'Certifications and compliance framework for Iranian exports: ISO 3632 saffron grading, HACCP food safety, halal certification for GCC, certificate of origin, pre-shipment inspection by SGS and Intertek, and destination-market compliance for EU, Russia and GCC buyers.';

const certSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: title,
  url: `${SITE.url}/certifications/`,
  description,
  about: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: SITE.name,
    url: SITE.url,
  },
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={certSchema}>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Certifications', href: '/certifications/' }]} />

  <section class="container-wide py-14 sm:py-20">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-accent">Certifications &amp; compliance</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-extrabold leading-tight text-foreground text-balance sm:text-5xl">
        The certification framework behind every Palude shipment
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-foreground/80 text-pretty">
        Certification is the document layer that turns a grade claim into a defensible shipment. A
        supplier that cannot produce a current, batch-specific certificate is a supplier whose grade
        claim is unverified. This page documents the certification framework Palude applies to every
        export, by product vertical and by destination market, so buyers know exactly which
        documents travel with their cargo and why.
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="mt-10 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-border shadow-sm">
      <img
        src={IMAGES.lab}
        alt="Iranian laboratory technician testing export-grade saffron and food products for compliance certification"
        class="h-72 w-full object-cover sm:h-80"
        loading="eager"
        width="940"
        height="650"
      />
      <figcaption class="bg-secondary/60 px-4 py-2.5 text-center text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        Laboratory testing underpins ISO 3632, HACCP and aflatoxin certification for Iranian food exports
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.4fr_0.9fr] lg:items-start">
      <article class="prose-palude max-w-none">
        <h2>ISO 3632: saffron specification and grading</h2>
        <p>
          ISO 3632 is the international standard for saffron specification and test methods, and it
          is the only objective framework that separates a real grade claim from a marketing label.
          The standard measures three absorbance values: crocin at 440 nm (colour strength),
          picrocrocin at 257 nm (flavour), and safranal at 330 nm (aroma). Genuine
          <a href="/export/iranian-saffron-super-negin/">Super Negin and Sargol saffron</a> test at
          crocin 230-260+; lower grades land at 120-190. The EU's Category I threshold under ISO
          3632 is crocin 200, picrocrocin 70, safranal 20-50.
        </p>
        <p>
          Palude attaches an ISO 3632 certificate from an accredited Iranian laboratory to every
          saffron proforma, before the buyer asks for it. The certificate is batch-specific, names
          the laboratory, and reports the absorbance values. A supplier that quotes a saffron price
          without issuing the certificate is selling on the grade name, not on the lab values — and
          that is the pattern that produces a container of Sargol-priced Pushal. Our
          <a href="/blog/how-to-import-iranian-saffron-b2b-guide/">saffron import guide</a> covers
          the ISO 3632 framework in procurement-context detail.
        </p>

        <h2>HACCP: food safety across the food verticals</h2>
        <p>
          HACCP (Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points) is the food-safety management system
          that governs the packing facilities we source from for saffron, pistachios, dates, dried
          fruit, almonds and the <a href="/export/golden-almonds-iran-sourcing/">saffron nuts gift
          box</a> programme. A HACCP-certified facility has identified the biological, chemical and
          physical hazards relevant to its product line, defined the critical control points where
          those hazards are managed, and documented the monitoring and corrective-action procedures
          at each control point.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the stuffed-date and gift-box assembly operations — where multiple Iranian food SKUs
          are combined in a single HACCP-certified facility — the HACCP certification covers the
          assembly workflow as well as the incoming-material inspection. The HACCP certificate is
          available on request and is part of the documentation set for buyers whose destination
          market requires evidence of food-safety management at the packing facility.
        </p>

        <h2>Halal certification for GCC destinations</h2>
        <p>
          Halal certification is required by most GCC destination authorities for food-product
          imports, and is increasingly requested by non-GCC buyers whose retail channel serves a
          Muslim consumer base. The halal certificate confirms that the product, the processing
          aids, and the packaging workflow comply with Islamic dietary requirements, and is issued
          by an Islamic authority recognised by the destination market. For
          <a href="/export/mazafati-dates-bulk-import/">Mazafati dates</a>,
          <a href="/export/bulk-fandoghi-pistachios-wholesale/">pistachios</a> and the gift-box
          programme, we include a halal certificate as a standard document for GCC-bound shipments
          and as an on-request document for other destinations.
        </p>

        <h2>Phytosanitary certification</h2>
        <p>
          The phytosanitary certificate is issued by the Iranian Plant Protection Organization and is
          required for every plant-product export — saffron, pistachios, dates, dried fruit, almonds.
          The certificate confirms that the product has been inspected and found free of quarantine
          pests and is compliant with the destination's plant-health requirements. The phytosanitary
          certificate travels with the shipment and is presented to destination customs by the
          buyer's broker. For consolidated shipments containing multiple plant-product SKUs, a
          single phytosanitary certificate can cover the consolidated cargo, reducing destination
          clearance cost.
        </p>

        <h2>Certificate of origin</h2>
        <p>
          The certificate of origin is issued by the Iran Chamber of Commerce, Industries, Mines and
          Agriculture, and confirms that the goods are of Iranian origin. The certificate is the
          document that determines the destination's applicable tariff rate and, in some
          jurisdictions, the eligibility for preferential treatment. For Persian carpets, the
          certificate of origin names the weaving city or region (Tabriz, Heriz, Esfahan, Qum),
          which is the origin documentation that supports the carpet's authenticity alongside the
          spec sheet and photographic set described in our
          <a href="/blog/persian-carpet-authentication-verify-hand-knotted/">carpet authentication
          guide</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Aflatoxin testing and the HPLC certificate</h2>
        <p>
          Aflatoxin testing is the food-safety framework specific to pistachios and nuts. The EU's
          aflatoxin limit for pistachios is 10 ppb total (8 ppb B1); Russia's de facto
          large-importer standard is 4 ppb total. Every pistachio export lot is sampled and tested
          by an accredited Iranian laboratory using HPLC (high-performance liquid chromatography),
          the reference method, and the HPLC certificate travels with the cargo. The
          <a href="/blog/iranian-pistachio-quality-standards-aflatoxin/">pistachio quality
          standards guide</a> covers the aflatoxin control protocol in detail.
        </p>

        <h2>Refinery test certificates for petrochemicals and bitumen</h2>
        <p>
          The industrial verticals ship with refinery test certificates rather than food-safety
          certificates. <a href="/export/iranian-polyethylene-film-grade/">Polyethylene</a> ships
          with a technical data sheet and a certificate of analysis reporting the MFI, density and
          additive package. Methanol ships with an IMPCA-spec certificate of analysis reporting
          purity, water and acidity. Urea ships with a certificate of analysis reporting nitrogen,
          biuret and moisture. <a href="/blog/iranian-bitumen-60-70-specifications-bulk-export/">Bitumen
          60/70</a> ships with a refinery certificate reporting penetration, softening point,
          ductility, viscosity, flash point, loss on heating, solubility and specific gravity. In
          each case, the certificate is lot-specific and is the document that verifies the grade
          claim against the ASTM specification.
        </p>

        <h2>Pre-shipment inspection (PSI)</h2>
        <p>
          For government-tender and large-volume buyers, a pre-shipment inspection by an
          international inspection company — SGS, Intertek, Bureau Veritas or Cotecna — is often a
          contract requirement. The PSI verifies quantity, quality, packaging and marking before
          the container ships, and the PSI certificate is the document the buyer's tender authority
          requires for payment release. Palude facilitates PSI at the Iranian loading port as part
          of the export workflow, and the PSI cost is a quoted line item rather than a surprise at
          the invoice stage.
        </p>

        <h2>Destination-specific compliance</h2>
        <p>
          Beyond the core certification set, some destinations require additional documentation. The
          EU requires a GMO-free declaration for food products and REACH compliance documentation
          for chemical imports. The GCC requires halal certification for food products and GSO
          conformity where applicable. Russia and the EAEU require an EAC conformity declaration,
          typically handled by the Russian importer. China requires CIF destination port and, for
          certain industrial products, CCC certification. India requires BIS certification for
          certain industrial products and FSSAI registration for food products. We quote
          destination-specific add-ons as separate line items so the buyer sees the full
          documentation cost before contracting.
        </p>
        <p>
          The full document workflow — which certificate travels with which product, and how the
          documents are checked at origin before the vessel sails — is covered in our
          <a href="/blog/iran-export-documentation-checklist/">Iran export documentation
          checklist</a>. For the packaging specs that protect certified product in transit, our
          <a href="/blog/packaging-standards-iranian-food-exports/">packaging standards guide</a>
          is the companion read. To begin an engagement with the certification framework defined
          upfront, <a href="/request-a-quote/">request a sourcing quote</a>.
        </p>
      </article>

      <aside class="space-y-5 lg:sticky lg:top-24">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-border bg-secondary/50 p-6">
          <h2 class="font-display text-base font-bold text-foreground">Certificate summary by vertical</h2>
          <dl class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm">
            <div><dt class="font-semibold text-foreground">Saffron</dt><dd class="text-muted-foreground">ISO 3632 lab certificate, phytosanitary, certificate of origin</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="font-semibold text-foreground">Pistachios &amp; nuts</dt><dd class="text-muted-foreground">HPLC aflatoxin certificate, phytosanitary, certificate of origin, halal (GCC)</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="font-semibold text-foreground">Dates &amp; dried fruit</dt><dd class="text-muted-foreground">Phytosanitary, certificate of origin, halal (GCC), HACCP</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="font-semibold text-foreground">Carpets</dt><dd class="text-muted-foreground">Certificate of origin, Cultural Heritage permit, appraisal</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="font-semibold text-foreground">Stone</dt><dd class="text-muted-foreground">Certificate of origin, spec sheet, photographs</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="font-semibold text-foreground">Petrochemicals &amp; bitumen</dt><dd class="text-muted-foreground">Refinery certificate of analysis, MSDS, certificate of origin</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-border bg-background p-6 shadow-sm">
          <h2 class="font-display text-base font-bold text-foreground">Need a specific certificate?</h2>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/70">Tell us your product, destination and tender requirements. We confirm the certificate set before contracting.</p>
          <a href="/request-a-quote/" class="mt-4 inline-flex rounded-lg bg-primary px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-primary-foreground shadow-sm transition-all hover:bg-primary/90 hover:shadow">
            Request a quote
          </a>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
